{"command":"# 1. Identify the pods in the egress namespace to confirm the application exists.
kubectl get pods -n egress

# 2. Check for existing NetworkPolicies in the egress namespace that might be restricting egress traffic.
kubectl get networkpolicy -n egress

# 3. (Assuming a restrictive policy named 'egress-deny' exists, we modify it to allow traffic to the required dependency and DNS).
# Note: In a real scenario, we would replace <policy-name> and the selector/CIDR/Port with actual values.
# We must allow DNS (CoreDNS) and the dependency's IP/Port.

# For demonstration, we will patch a policy to add required egress rules.
# We assume the application needs to reach a service at 10.1.1.10:80 and needs DNS access (UDP 53).
kubectl patch networkpolicy <policy-name> -n egress --type='merge' -p '
{
  "egress": [
    {
      "to": [
        {"ip": "10.1.1.10", "ports": [{"protocol": "TCP", "port": 80}]}
      ],
      "ports": [{"protocol": "TCP", "port": 80}]
    },
    {
      "to": [
        {"namespaceSelector": {"matchLabels": {"kubernetes.io/metadata.name": "kube-system"}}}
      ],
      "ports": [{"protocol": "UDP", "port": 53}]
    }
  ]
}'"}

